10:46:08 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=0 miss=307
10:46:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not necessarily. Antivirus alerts mean “this file is suspicious or matches a known threat pattern,” not always “this is proven malicious beyond doubt.” Detection can come from different sources: - **Known malware signatures:** Often high confidence, but even these can occasionally match legitimate files or modified software. - **Behavior-based detection:** A program may do something malware commonly does—modify startup settings, encrypt many files, inject into another process—but legitimate admin, backup, security, or development tools may do similar things. - **Heuristics and machine learning:** These deliberately err somewhat on the side of caution, so they can produce false positives. - **Potentially unwanted programs:** Toolbars, bundled installers, crack tools, and similar software may be unwanted or risky without being straightforward malware. Quarantine is therefore a safer default than deletion: it stops the file from executing immediately, but preserves the ability to restore it if the detection was wrong, investigate an incident, or recover an important file. For a clearly confirmed threat, antivirus software may automatically delete it—or offer deletion after quarantine—but quarantine provides a useful safety net.
