10:46:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Neither. A “threat detected” message is generally a truthful statement that the antivirus detected something it classifies as a security threat or potential threat under its rules. That classification can be highly reliable without being infallible. This is common in safety systems: - A smoke alarm can truthfully report smoke even if the cause is burnt toast rather than a house fire. - A bank can flag a transaction as potentially fraudulent without claiming fraud is proven. - An antivirus can flag a file as malware-like, risky, unwanted, or matching a detection signature without claiming that a human has conclusively established malicious intent. Calling it a threat is not a promise of 100% certainty; it communicates the recommended safety posture: **do not trust or run it.** Quarantine enforces that posture immediately while preserving the file in case the classification needs correction.
